   "In a 2D closed room the fan's speeds are qualitative and grid-dependent: speeds, the disc velocity, volume flow and convective heat transfer have no grid-converged answer (for a 1 N/m fan in a 4 × 3 m room the mean speed is 2.4 m/s at 6 and 12 cells per fan width and 3.4 m/s at 24, and the room needs 500–900 L/U to settle; theory_ns2d.md §18.4). The thrust is exact and the flow pattern is meaningful. The fan's power can only leave through wall friction and numerical dissipation (2D flow has no vortex stretching), so the room spins up into room-sized vortices. Use 3D for rooms. This run's 240 L/U end inside that start-up transient, so its averages are one sample of it.",
